{
 "id": "goal-relevance",
 "kicker": "What matters",
 "title": "Imitate what serves the jump",
 "tagline": "Arm-waving added before the jump gets the minimum weight (0.10, the same as standing still) while the takeoff push and the landing peak at 0.97–1.00.",
 "story": {
  "shown": "The squat-jump reference with a distraction injected: from 0.2 to 1.4 s both arms go overhead and pump, like someone hyping a crowd before a jump. The jump itself is unchanged.",
  "learned": "For every frame and body part, how much it matters for the goal. The goal comes from the action's stages: lower the centre of mass, push off, fly, absorb the landing. Credit is split by physics: the change in each goal quantity is divided exactly among the body parts that caused it, and back-and-forth motion cancels.",
  "how": "In the waving window the arms move 4.0× faster than they do for the rest of the clip, yet the frame weight stays at the floor (0.10). The legs get 91% of the takeoff credit, the arms 5%. After the waving the weights are identical to the clean clip's. Training scales the imitation (style) terms by this weight, so the policy isn't paid to copy the waving."
 },
